// A TextFile may not be released by a sweep, because the runtime keeps every
// one of them on a list it walks at exit.
//
// A handle joins `all_files` when it is MADE, not when it is opened, so this
// needs no file on disk.  Releasing one left it linked there with its `next`
// overwritten by the free list's link, and `close_open_files` then walked in a
// circle: every pass found the handle already nil, did nothing, and went round
// again.  It cost twenty-six minutes of spin on one run.
//
// So the failure this case guards against is a HANG AT EXIT rather than a wrong
// answer, which is why the harness bounds how long a case may take.  It was
// also invisible under ALG_POISON=1, where a body is never handed back out and
// the list stays intact -- the harness runs both ways for that reason.

procedure Churn ();
begin
    for var I : Integer := 0; I < 4000; I := I + 1 do
    begin
        var Junk := [I, I, I];
    end
end

begin
    // Abandoned immediately: nothing refers to any of them afterwards, and the
    // LAST one made is at the head of `all_files`.
    for var I : Integer := 0; I < 400; I := I + 1 do
    begin
        var Handle := TextFile ();
    end

    // Churned so that there is ordinary garbage for the sweep to find, and the
    // count below is asserting that a collection happened rather than
    // asserting the exemption -- which would turn red for an implementation
    // that unlinked a released handle instead of exempting it, a change this
    // case has no business forbidding.
    Churn ();

    WriteLn ('released something: ' + Str (Collect () > 0));

    // ⚠️ MORE HANDLES, and this is the whole reproduction.  A released body
    // only does damage when it is handed back to an object OF ITS OWN SIZE
    // CLASS, and a List is a different class from a TextFile -- so churning
    // Lists here reproduced nothing and the case passed with the bug present.
    //
    // A new handle takes a released body and runs `next := all_files`.  If the
    // released body is still the head of that list, which the last one made
    // is, the result is a handle whose `next` is itself: a self-loop, and
    // `close_open_files` never reaches the end of it.
    for var I : Integer := 0; I < 400; I := I + 1 do
    begin
        var Another := TextFile ();
    end

    var Kept := TextFile ();

    WriteLn ('kept a handle: ' + Str (Kept is TextFile));
    WriteLn ('reached the end');
end
